.TH SCHVIEW 1
.SH NAME
schview \- show the pages of a schematic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B schview
.I sheet.vec
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Schview
shows the pages of a schematic in a window, one at a time, smoothed,
on black: the text and the sheet's frame and title block (the records
from a page record to its first part or net) khaki (#BBBB99), the rest
of the drawing a whiter gray (#D4D4D0).
A page runs from its page record (page W H N) to the next; a part newly
selected, from stdin or by a click, shows its page; PgDn and PgUp turn
the pages with a part selected too.
The window's title is the absolute path of the file.
The sheet comes as
.IR vec (5)
from
.IR schdraw (1);
its text is in the stroke font of
.IR vec (5),
no stroke thinner than a pixel, and everything is drawn by OpenGL, smoothed by multisampling and its lines
shaded by their distance, in linear light.
.PP
It opens fitted to the sheet, as Space fits it; the wheel zooms round
the pointer and the left button drags the view.
A click within 3 pixels of the box of an element of a part (its
symbol's body, or one of its texts: designator, value, pin numbers)
selects the part, of the smallest such element under the pointer, and
prints
.B select
.I NAME
on stdout; a click on none clears the selection here alone and prints
nothing.
A click within 3 pixels of a wire, a junction dot or a pin's end, out
of any part (a part wins), selects the net: its wires, dots and flags
with their names are drawn light cyan and its name stands in a black
box in the upper right corner, after its class and a colon if its net
record gives one
.RI ( vec (5)),
as power:outputs.ch1.gain.vp; Esc clears it, and so does a click on
nothing.
A click on a global's mark or name selects its net.
A click on a port's circle or name goes to the first page of its list
other than the one shown, centered on its port there at the same zoom;
a click on a page number of its list goes to that page; on its own
number, a bracket or a comma, nothing.
The selected part, its symbol, pins and labels, is drawn light cyan
(#A0FFFF): its relative luminance, 0.86, is 1.8 times the khaki's, 0.48,
so the change shows without color too.
Each note of the sheet is a khaki box with the letter N in it; the
pointer over it opens it: the note's text shows in a black box at the
upper left of the window, wrapped at about 60 characters and inside the
window, and its box and N, and every part of the note's group (its name the group, or the
group and a dot before the rest), magenta (#FF80FF), so the note open
shows among the others; all go when the pointer leaves the box,
and the selection stays as it was.
The texts over the drawing (the note open, the net's name, Loading...)
are in the stroke font, their capitals 14 pixels high and the pen 1.5
wide, whatever the zoom; the help is in the font fixed.
While g and a page number are typed, the top left shows "go to: N".
The keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
c           center the selected part; again, with nothing
            changed in between, zoom in two times about it
C           zoom out two times about the middle of the window,
            centering nothing
Space       fit the sheet: its frame at the edge of the window
F5          read the file again, the view kept; Loading...
            in the upper right corner meanwhile
Esc         deselect, here alone; hide the help
?           a help of the keys, on and off
q           quit
.fi
.RE
.PP
It reads commands on stdin, a line each:
.B select
.I NAME
selects,
.B select
clears (for scripts: the viewers never send it), and
.B center
is c;
.B fit
as Space,
.B reload
as F5;
.B shot
.I file
draws the next frame into a PPM picture;
.BR "page next" ,
.B page prev
and
.B page
.I N
turn the pages.
The input of the window is taken before each command, so a picture
shows all the keys and clicks that came before it.
Cross-probing with
.IR pcbview (1)
is two FIFOs, each viewer's stdout the other's stdin.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
schdraw ch1.sch > ch1.vec && schview ch1.vec
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
pcbview(1), schdraw(1), vec(5)
